  :adr/status :accepted
  :adr/title "kotoba-lang/face-liveness: challenge-response liveness via real EAR/MAR/yaw geometry, no bundled detector"
  :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/face-liveness]
  :adr/related ["orgs/kotoba-lang/ekyc" "orgs/kotoba-lang/mrz" "orgs/kotoba-lang/watchlist-screen"
                "orgs/kotoba-lang/inference"]
  :adr/context
  "Phase C of the eKYC/AML vendor plan approved earlier this session
   (following Phase A kotoba-lang/mrz, ADR-2607198200, and Phase B
   kotoba-lang/watchlist-screen, ADR-2607198500). ekyc.model/checks
   includes :liveness as a check kind ekyc's own consumer can request;
   nothing in this workspace implemented it.

   Face-liveness detection (proving a live human, not a static photo, is
   in front of the camera) is a genuinely hard, actively-researched CV
   problem at the commercial-vendor level (3D depth sensing, texture
   analysis, deep anti-spoofing models). Building or claiming that level
   of robustness from scratch in this session would be exactly the
   false-confidence failure mode the whole eKYC/AML effort is structured
   to avoid (per ADR-2607198200's framing). But a NARROWER, real,
   classical technique exists and is buildable honestly: challenge-
   response liveness via facial landmark motion analysis (ask the subject
   to blink/turn/smile, verify the requested motion actually happened in a
   tracked frame sequence). The core geometric primitive, Eye Aspect Ratio
   (EAR), is a real, published, peer-reviewed formula (Soukupová & Čech,
   'Real-Time Eye Blink Detection Using Facial Landmarks', 2016) widely
   reproduced across open-source blink-detection implementations -- this
   is legitimately real math, not invented for this repo.

   What this repo does NOT attempt: a face-landmark DETECTOR (the CV model
   that turns a camera image into landmark point coordinates in the first
   place). kotoba-lang/inference's own README states the org's stance
   plainly: 'JavaScript model runtimes such as Transformers.js, ONNX, and
   ONNX Runtime Web are not portable foundations for this repo' -- the
   sanctioned path for any vision workload is torch+num, which today has
   zero vision-workload precedent (its one working inference path is
   GGUF/Gemma LLM-shaped). Building a from-scratch landmark detector on an
   unproven vision-workload foundation, with no labeled face data to
   validate against, would be the same false-confidence risk this ADR
   otherwise avoids -- so face-liveness.ports/ILandmarkTracker ships as a
   clean host-injection seam with NO implementation, honestly."
  :adr/decision
  "Add kotoba-lang/face-liveness, a portable .cljc library:

   - face-liveness.model -- challenge lifecycle (issue/expire, TTL-based),
     the neutral named-point landmark-frame shape (NOT positional indices
     into one detector's specific point-numbering scheme -- a real
     ILandmarkTracker maps its own output into this shape, decoupling the
     tested scoring math from any one detector), result records
     (:non-adjudicating true always, mirroring ekyc/aml/watchlist-screen's
     existing convention).
   - face-liveness.geometry -- Eye Aspect Ratio (the real Soukupová & Čech
     formula: EAR = (‖p2-p6‖+‖p3-p5‖)/(2·‖p1-p4‖) over 6 eye-contour
     points), Mouth Aspect Ratio (the identical geometric shape applied to
     a mouth contour -- a documented, common, but not independently
     peer-reviewed simplification), and a yaw proxy (an EXPLICITLY LABELED
     dimensionless [-1 1] nose/eye horizontal-symmetry heuristic, not a
     real 3D pose estimate -- a genuine head-pose solve needs a calibrated
     camera model and PnP, out of scope for six 2D points). All three
     propagate nil on a missing point rather than guessing.
   - face-liveness.core -- per-challenge-type detection requiring a REAL
     TRANSITION, not a single threshold crossing: :blink needs a
     dip-then-recover EAR curve (open->closed->open, with the open state
     observed on both sides of the dip -- rules out a sequence that starts
     already closed or never reopens), :smile needs a below-then-above MAR
     transition (rules out someone already smiling at frame 1), :turn-left/
     :turn-right need a centered-then-committed yaw transition in the
     correct direction (rules out someone starting already turned). This
     transition requirement is deliberate: a single-frame or single-value
     check would be trivially satisfiable by a static, already-posed
     photo, defeating the entire point of a liveness challenge.
   - face-liveness.ports/ILandmarkTracker -- the camera/CV seam, protocol
     only, no implementation (see Context).
   - face-liveness.datom -- audit emitters mirroring ekyc.datom's exact
     shape (pure functions, flat-namespaced-attribute datom vectors).

   24 tests / 40 assertions covering EAR/MAR formula correctness against
   hand-computed synthetic geometry, yaw-proxy sign/symmetry, every
   challenge type's POSITIVE and NEGATIVE detection path (a real motion
   pattern verifies; noise, a static pose, or the wrong turn direction
   fails), and expiry vs. empty-frames as distinct, non-conflated
   outcomes. clj-kondo: 0 errors, 0 warnings."
  :adr/consequences
  ["Any eKYC consumer (the planned kotoba-lang/ekyc-native-provider, Phase E) gets a real, tested :liveness check implementation instead of ekyc's own dead-end -- ekyc.model/checks already names :liveness as a requestable check kind with nothing behind it."
   "The honest capability boundary is explicit in every doc: defends against a static printed photo or single still image; does NOT defend against pre-recorded video replay, a 3D mask, or deepfake/video-injection feeding a virtual camera. A caller cannot mistake this for commercial-grade anti-spoofing."
   "No ILandmarkTracker ships -- a real integration must supply a face-landmark detector itself (an external model, a native host capability, or a future torch+num vision extension this ADR does not attempt). This repo is unusable end-to-end without that piece; it is the tested motion-detection math on top of it."
   "The yaw-proxy left/right sign convention is documented as an unverified calibration risk (camera mirroring varies) -- a real integration must confirm it against its own capture pipeline, not assume this repo got it right."]
  :adr/references [2607198200 2607198500]}]
